## Claim
Text: "In England You can get a £3,000 fine for not wearing a cloth over your face at the time of this claim."
Claim author: Jessica Shelley Monk
Claim date: September 30, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### What specific law or public health regulation in England mandated the wearing of a face covering in public spaces during the COVID-19 pandemic?
Answer: The specific regulation was the **Health Protection (Coronavirus, Wearing of Face Coverings in a Relevant Place) (England) Regulations 2020 (SI 2020/791)**, a statutory instrument brought into force on 23 July 2020 by the Secretary of State for Health and Social Care using emergency powers under the Public Health (Control of Disease) Act 1984. It mandated face coverings in specified indoor venues like shops and public transport hubs, with a fixed penalty notice of £100 for non-compliance, later increased to a minimum of £200 and a maximum of £3,200 for repeat offenders.

Source URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Health_Protection_(Coronavirus,_Wearing_of_Face_Coverings_in_a_Relevant_Place)_(England)_Regulations_2020

### Was the legal requirement to wear a face covering in England in effect on September 30, 2020?
Answer: Yes, the legal requirement to wear a face covering in certain settings in England was in effect on September 30, 2020. According to the source, the Health Protection (Coronavirus, Wearing of Face Coverings in a Relevant Place) (England) Regulations 2020 "came into force" on 24 July 2020, and if approved by both Houses of Parliament (which had to occur before 21 September 2020 to avoid the regulations lapsing), they were set to "expire a year after coming into force." Since the regulations remained in effect and were approved, the face covering requirement was indeed legally in force on September 30, 2020, with a penalty for non-compliance of "a fine of £100, reduced to £50 if paid within two weeks."

Source URL: https://lordslibrary.parliament.uk/covid-19-regulations-face-coverings-in-shops/

### On what date did the requirement to wear face coverings in certain settings in England first come into effect?
Answer: The requirement to wear face coverings in shops and other specified places in England first came into force on **24 July 2020**, when the Health Protection (Coronavirus, Wearing of Face Coverings in a Relevant Place) (England) Regulations 2020 took effect. However, face coverings had earlier become mandatory on public transport in England on **15 June 2020**.

Source URL: https://lordslibrary.parliament.uk/covid-19-regulations-face-coverings-in-shops/

### What was the prescribed legal penalty or fine for individuals who failed to wear a required face covering in England in September 2020?
Answer: In September 2020, individuals in England who refused to wear a required face covering faced an initial fixed penalty notice of £100, reducible to £50 if paid within 14 days; for repeated offenses, the fine could double with each subsequent breach, up to a maximum of £3,200.

Source URL: https://jerseyeveningpost.com/morenews/uknews/2020/08/14/new-powers-to-tackle-coronavirus-mask-refusers/

### Was the maximum fine for non-compliance with face covering rules in England £3,000 during September 2020?
Answer: No, the maximum fine was not exactly £3,000. According to the source, the fine for a first refusal was £100, which would double for subsequent offences, up to a maximum of **£3,200**. The claim of £3,000 is close but not precise—the actual maximum was £3,200. These enhanced penalties were announced in mid-August 2020 and would have been in effect during September 2020.

Source URL: https://jerseyeveningpost.com/morenews/uknews/2020/08/14/new-powers-to-tackle-coronavirus-mask-refusers/

### Did the £3,000 figure represent a fixed penalty notice amount or the maximum fine upon prosecution in a magistrates' court?
Answer: The £3,000 figure in the claim likely refers to the maximum fine upon prosecution for repeated offences, not a fixed penalty notice amount. The search result explains that while the initial fixed penalty for not wearing a mask was £100, "that penalty will double for subsequent offences, up to a maximum of £3,200."

Source URL: https://www.jerseyeveningpost.com/morenews/uknews/2020/08/13/new-powers-to-tackle-coronavirus-mask-refusers-as-englands-lockdown-eases/

### Which specific settings or locations in England were subject to the mandatory face covering rule in September 2020?
Answer: Based on the source, in September 2020, face coverings were mandatory in England in **shops and supermarkets** (since 24 July 2020), on **public transport**, and at **NHS facilities** (since 15 June 2020). The article states, "Wearing a face covering in shops and supermarkets in England is to become mandatory from 24 July," and "Mask-wearing has been compulsory on public transport in England and at NHS facilities across the UK since 15 June."

Source URL: https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-politics-53397617

### Were there any legally recognized exemptions from the face covering requirement in England at that time?
Answer: Yes, there were legally recognized exemptions. The regulations specified that children under 11, people working in shops, and those with "reasonable excuses" (such as accompanying someone who relies on lipreading) were exempt from the face covering requirement.

Source URL: https://lordslibrary.parliament.uk/covid-19-regulations-face-coverings-in-shops/

### Did the face covering requirement in England specify that the covering must be made of cloth, or did it allow other types of masks?
Answer: The face covering requirement in England allowed other types of masks, as the regulation defined a face covering simply as "anything which covers the mouth and nose," with no specification regarding material such as cloth.

Source URL: https://lordslibrary.parliament.uk/covid-19-regulations-face-coverings-in-shops/

### Who was the authorized enforcement body (e.g., police, transport staff) responsible for issuing fines for non-compliance with face covering rules in England in September 2020?
Answer: Based on the search result, the authorized enforcement body responsible for issuing fines for non-compliance with face covering rules in England was the **police**. The article states that "if you don’t cooperate with the police and if you put our health at risk, action will follow," and notes that "police chiefs have promised increased patrols."

Source URL: https://jerseyeveningpost.com/morenews/uknews/2020/08/14/new-powers-to-tackle-coronavirus-mask-refusers/

## Final Judgement
Based on the evidence gathered, the fact-check establishes that while face coverings were legally required in England in September 2020 under the Health Protection (Coronavirus, Wearing of Face Coverings in a Relevant Place) (England) Regulations 2020, the prescribed penalty was an initial fixed penalty of £100, doubling for repeat offenses up to a maximum of £3,200—not precisely £3,000. Furthermore, the regulation defined a face covering broadly as "anything which covers the mouth and nose," with no requirement for it to be made of cloth.

The information needed to finalize the fact-check is now complete, as the evidence directly addresses the claim's key elements: the existence of the mandate, the date of enforcement, and the specifics of the fine and covering material.

The claim is `refuted` because the evidence explicitly contradicts its core assertions: the maximum fine was £3,200, not £3,000, and the law did not specify that the covering must be cloth.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is refuted as it inaccurately states the penalty and the type of face covering required. Under the **Health Protection (Coronavirus, Wearing of Face Coverings in a Relevant Place) (England) Regulations 2020**, which were in effect in September 2020, the penalty for non-compliance was an initial fine of £100, which could double for repeated offences up to a maximum of £3,200, not £3,000. Furthermore, the law did not specify that the covering must be cloth, defining a face covering only as anything that covers the mouth and nose.